# Three-module Lean repair

## Objective and acceptance contract

Repair the historical `RodCircles`, `RodScaling`, and `TowerCollapse` proof builds.
Their original mathematical statements and hypotheses MUST be preserved. Smaller
lemmas and bounded finite computations MAY replace expensive proof bodies.
Success SHALL require fresh Lean compilation, complete theorem assumption audits,
source/artifact hashes, and rejection controls. A successful process exit alone
MUST NOT be reported as proof acceptance.

These are number-theory models used by the miner research project. Passing them
does not prove a Bitcoin nonce-pruning advantage, a faster SHA-256 algorithm, or
refinement of all miner runtime code.

## Read in this order

1. [Historical outcome](../miner-proof-retry-20261007/final-review.json): the failed
   bounded attempts, preserved unchanged.
2. [Source manifest](source-manifest.json): exact historical and repaired inputs.
3. [RodCircles](sources/RequestProject/RodCircles.lean): finite-field conics and CRT
   multiplication of point counts. Its final proof MUST retain the multiplication
   expression shape documented in the repair contract.
4. [RodScaling](sources/RequestProject/RodScaling.lean): three exact finite counts
   at moduli 121 and 361, with smaller counting steps linked to `formPts`.
5. [TowerCollapse](sources/RequestProject/TowerCollapse.lean): exponent periods,
   three/four-level tower reduction, and the 72 distinct finite values.
6. [Build gate](run_retry.py): fresh local import closure, allowed axiom policy,
   exact named-root coverage, generated-root audit, failure controls, bindings.
7. [API review](API_SUPPORT.md): live formula discovery and supporting scalar
   calculations. Provider status flags MUST NOT substitute for Lean proofs.

## Workflow

`run_file.py` is a bounded diagnostic compiler. Its successful exit MAY guide a
repair but MUST NOT set final acceptance. Files such as `CircleBase.lean` and
`CircleCounts.lean` isolate failures; only the original module targets and their
fresh dependency closure count toward the final repair gate.

`prepare_manifest.py` verifies that every original theorem header is retained,
binds the supporting API scalar values, and records the current candidate inputs.
Run it only when intentionally preparing a new candidate revision. After that,
`run_retry.py` SHALL reject changes to the recorded sources.

The full gate command is `make check`. Its stages are:

```sh
python3 -B prepare_manifest.py
python3 -B run_retry.py --target RequestProject.RodCircles --target RequestProject.RodScaling --target RequestProject.TowerCollapse --target RequestProject.ApiSupportNumbers --timeout 180 --memory-mb 3072
python3 -B run_lake.py --timeout 180
python3 -B write_review.py
```

`run_lake.py` creates another fresh project from the exact accepted source bytes.
Lake builds the local dependency closure, and a second assumption audit MUST
match the raw Lean audit. Pinned third-party compiled prerequisites are reused;
this is not a fresh build of Mathlib. `make verify` checks existing bound evidence.

Each attempt has its own output directory. Historical failures and reference
sources MUST remain available. Final review MUST distinguish compiled diagnostic
candidates from a successful source-bound gate.

## Findings established so far

The old blanket import-pressure diagnosis was incomplete. A process sample
located the stall in kernel definitional equality. The final RodCircles proof
used raw `Nat.mul` congruences against overloaded multiplication in its types;
the resulting comparison reduced enormous finite counts. Matching the symbolic
multiplication expression makes this proof check without enumerating those sets.

The API documentation and formula catalog were checked live. No new Lean proof
endpoint or prime-square conic lifting theorem was found. Five supporting integer
products agreed with independent arithmetic; their exact returned values are
also included in the local Lean gate.
